   "transcript": "Voice-over: Hello! Did you grow up with a mother or father who had a different mother tongue from you? What’s it like to have a language barrier between you and your parents?\nNext stop, a busy German bakery full of tempting rye bread and strudel, to hear how language has shifted between the generations in writer and photographer Julian Lassi’s family.\n[Example]\nJulian Lassi: My parents are German and I was brought up speaking English and not German, and I was never taught it as a kid.\n[Question 1]\nJulian Lassi: That’s right, yeah. He was half-English, his mum was English and he had this real problem that he could never decide what language he dreamt in, so he would have a dream and wake up and say ‘I don’t know what language I dreamt in,’ and his big thing was about not being rooted in one place or not having a sense of where he was from, not having a home.\n[Question 2]\nJulian Lassi: My mum, she grew up speaking only German.\n[Question 3]\nJulian Lassi: They speak when we’re not in the room. When they were talking on the phone, they always spoke German and when they were talking about my birthday or about Christmas presents, they would always speak German but at all other times, they would speak English except when my grandmothers were there. They’d speak German to them.\n[Question 4]\nJulian Lassi: Well, my dad left Germany because he hated Germans. His reasons were really complex but it was basically he always said just after the war, he’d be sitting on a train and he wouldn’t know if the person opposite him was a Nazi or not.\n[Question 5]\nJulian Lassi: I think I’m 19 and I’m sitting in the car with my dad and I’m arguing with him, and the argument is about me not learning German when I was a kid, and I remember saying to him, ‘Why didn’t you teach me? If you’d taught me German, I would know it by now.’ And I remember being, feeling really angry that I’d been denied this opportunity.\n[Question 6]\nJulian Lassi: I was in Berlin for two years. Yeah, I got pretty fluent but you know, for quite a few months I wasn’t really progressing in my German and it was because when I was growing up, I could never understand what my parents were saying, I would always automatically switch off when I heard them. It was kind of a little click in my ear and I still have it sometimes, even now, but I mean back then it was a real problem because my ear was just tuning out.\n[Question 7]\nJulian Lassi: I lived with this German guy when I first moved to Berlin and he’d been brought up speaking English and German. His father was English, his mother was German but his English was really odd and he’d never actually been to England except on a school trip. But he was completely fluent, but his English was really odd. It was just off slightly.\n[Question 8]\nJulian Lassi: I always grew up thinking the bilingual thing was a good thing and now I wonder whether it wasn’t actually a good thing to grow up speaking just one language."
