   "transcript": "[Example]\nJournalist 1: These are Asian-Australian students being taught to learn Japanese. It’s the most widely studied foreign language in Australia. In recent years, there has been a surge in demand for Mandarin Chinese but the teaching of other Asian languages in schools here has been in decline. To some, that’s evidence of a growing parochialism in national life and a certain reluctance to become fully ‘Asia literate’.\n[News item 1]\nJournalist 2: From insects to exotic plants, all around the world nature is in dramatic decline. It’s the focus of a major new UN report warning that the planet’s facing an ecological crisis. And it’s humans who are to blame. Areas once rich in nature are being cleared for development, illegal trade is bringing animals to the brink of extinction and pollution is poisoning the seas.\n[News item 2]\nJournalist 3: The English countryside is changing. Amidst the wheat and barley, a new crop is on the rise. Vines this year across the region were planted in greater numbers than ever. English still or sparkling wine was once something of a novelty but drinkers say it easily holds its own place in the market. Last year’s long hot summer broke all records. With good weather forecast for the next few days, this year’s crop is likely to be more evidence that wine production is here to stay.\n[News item 3]\nJournalist 4: Once considered by many as the pastime of rebellious teenagers, skateboarding is shaking off its former image. Those in the industry say increasing numbers of adults across the capital are taking up skateboarding.\n[News item 4]\nJournalist 5: Australia has been pulled in all sorts of different directions. There’s still the constitutional and sentimental attachment to Britain; the Queen is still the head of state. There’s the defence partnership with America; Canberra likes to see itself as Washington’s most loyal ally. But increasingly there’s this commercial rapport with Beijing – China’s the country’s biggest trading partner. From an economic perspective, Australia’s future lies very much in Asia.\n[News item 5]\nJournalist 6: They have each already run over 200 marathons so Eleanor and her husband, Dennis, have plenty of fond memories, even if she wasn’t so keen at first. The pair only took up marathon running in their Fifties, something to keep them fit, they could enjoy as a couple.\n[News item 6]\nJournalist 7: This is Miriwoong country: a land of vast open skies that straddles modern-day state boundaries. The Miriwoong people have been here for tens of thousands of years but in recent decades, their language has come under threat. Before European settlement, Australia was home to around 300 indigenous languages. Now only a dozen are left with more than a thousand speakers.\n[News item 7]\nJournalist 8: A thriving puffin population. Skomer Island is renowned for its wide range of seabirds but the number of puffins breeding here is attracting international interest. They’ve been in decline in other parts of the UK and Europe for decades, so much so that the species is now endangered. Here scientists have found it’s a different picture."
