   "transcript": "Voice-over: Dogs and wolves are still the same species today. They can easily interbreed. Overall, wolves and dogs are 99.8% genetically identical. Given they are biologically so similar, is it the way we raise them in our homes that makes a dog? Scientists in Hungary set out to answer this question.\nScientist: We wanted to see whether the special relationship between humans and dogs are due to nature or nurture, so we wanted to see what happens if a wolf is raised in a human environment, in a home, whether it would act like a dog or not.\nVoice-over: A litter of five-day-old wolf cubs was taken from a wolf sanctuary outside Budapest. A group of young researchers became their adoptive parents, caring for them 24 hours a day. As a control for the experiment, they’d already raised puppies. Now they aim to raise the wolf cubs the same way.\nScientist: So we were especially nice with our cubs because we wanted to maintain a very good relationship with them.\nVoice-over: But then something began to change. Despite raising the cubs in the same way as the puppies, by eight weeks the differences had started to show.\nScientist: Dog puppies were always interested in what I was doing. There is a very strong co-operative tendency in dogs and this was missing in wolves. They had their own ideas. They were not much interested in my activities.\nVoice-over: The researchers wanted to find out exactly what was going on and decided to run a series of tests, comparing the wolf cubs with puppies of the same age.\nUnlike dogs, the wolf cubs didn’t respond to pointing. In fact, they hardly made eye contact with humans at all. The cubs were behaving as they would do in the wild.\nScientist: She was really possessive. If she wanted to grab an object, it was really difficult to get it back and if we wanted to open the refrigerator and have breakfast, the pup was immediately in the middle of the refrigerator and grabbed something. It is not like with a dog, that you say no, you shouldn’t. It just didn’t care.\nVoice-over: The battles continued to get worse.\nScientist: After the second month, we started to have more and more conflicts and the wolves wanted to destroy everything. And of course when the cub is a small cub, it’s nothing but when they reach 40 or 50 kilogrammes, you know, it starts to be really dangerous. We just could not keep them in the house any more.\nVoice-over: After four months, the cubs had to be returned to the reserve. The experiment had proved that upbringing has little impact. It’s impossible to turn a wolf into a dog, no matter how much you nurture it.\nScientist: So according to our experiences, the dog is not a socialised wolf at all. These differences we experienced in the cognitive ability and in the social behaviour of dogs, this is the effect of domestication.\nVoice-over: The difference must lie in the way dogs have been bred by humans over thousands of years. Their unique abilities are now part of their nature."
