   "transcript": "Rebecca Kesby: Now it’s time to Witness History with me, Rebecca Kesby, and today we explore a society that had a profound effect on Europe between the 8th and 11th centuries. Often depicted as marauding savages in the old history books, it wasn’t until the 1970s with the discovery in northern England that we understood more about the sophisticated culture of the Vikings.\nDr Peter Addyman: Curiously enough, the word Viking does something for people. They have this violent reputation. They were charismatic, something which appeals.\nProf Julian Richards: The popular image was that Vikings were a war-like people. What Coppergate showed was a totally different side.\nRebecca Kesby: In the 1970s, Dr Peter Addyman was director of the York Archaeological Trust and Professor Julian Richards was a post-graduate archaeologist. For many years it had been known that the northern English city of York had been an important settlement for the Vikings but virtually nothing was known about how they lived. Early Vikings had set sail from Denmark and Norway in the 700s to seek their fortunes. Looting and raiding along the coasts of modern-day Britain, France and Ireland, some went as far as Spain, Russia and the Middle East. They terrified locals and built a well-earned reputation for violence.\nBut in 1972, when a pub was demolished for redevelopment in the centre of York, archaeologists were allowed to inspect what lay beneath the pub cellars and, as Peter Addyman describes, it revealed an altogether more gentle side to the Vikings.\nDr Peter Addyman: We found underneath those cellars the wooden walls of Viking-age houses standing nearly two metres high and the place had been a shoe factory and we were finding offcuts of leather, complete shoes, even a last for making shoes.\nRebecca Kesby: What was the moment where you looked at each other and thought ‘Wow,’ you know, ‘this is even better than we’d hoped’?\nDr Peter Addyman: It was quite incredible. Everything was preserved there and that really was amazing actually, and I suppose the first thing was a bit of panic because once you expose these things to the air, they start to deteriorate. But then of course a marvellous feeling of ‘I’ve hit the bonanza here’, and very exciting.\nRebecca Kesby: Later on in the 1970s, the nearby site of Coppergate was also cleared for redevelopment. What archaeologists found there, almost perfectly preserved in York’s damp peaty soil, was an entire Viking-era street dominated, it seems, by metal workers. More crafts and industry were uncovered, a weaver’s shop, a textile dying factory and signs of a developed and organised town plan.\nDr Peter Addyman: Even the streets have got Viking names here. They all end in the suffix -gate which is Scandinavian gatan, which means ‘street’ so we have Copper-gate. It was the cup-makers’ street. There’s Mikelgate which is the great street and so forth. Then they subdivided them in long narrow strips and put the shop on the street front, workshops behind and so they were quite capable of building a really decent planned town.\nRebecca Kesby: It was in 866 that a Viking army invaded and finally took over York, or Yorvik as they called it, which was originally built as a walled Roman fortress town but by then was occupied by Anglo-Saxons. Following a pagan philosophy, the Vikings struggled with Christian England. The fact that they’d wrecked several monasteries and churches in search of treasure was rather awkward when it came to assimilating with the locals, but Peter and Julian say there are signs they tried to."
