   "transcript": "Voice-over: Being a graffiti artist is illegal and often dangerous, so why do so many people do it? Mike Williams tries to find out.\nMike Williams: This modern style of graffiti, all colour and curving shapes or stark jagged letters, this style has its origins in New York City in the 1970s when the subway trains were daubed with paint. It’s been adopted by graffiti writers around the world but graffiti itself is a very, very old thing.\nProf Richard Clay: So, the Oxford English Dictionary definition of graffiti says it’s marks that are left on walls illicitly.\nMike Williams: Professor Richard Clay, an Art historian at Newcastle University.\nProf Richard Clay: The illicitness is really important to setting acceptable art apart from that which is counter to official acceptance. We could trace it all the way back over thirty thousand years ago. The Grottes d’Arcy which are in Burgundy in France, these are images and signs that are left. Some of them are clearly approved of by the community but there’s the occasional mark which looks to be illicit.\nMike Williams: What kind of mark?\nProf Richard Clay: It’s a hand mark. It’s a stencil and it looks like a kid might have blown paint over their own hand to leave a mark. It was quite a revelation to see this hand. It’s just haunting, you know this tiny little hand left by this child so, so long ago. It’s such a connection with humanity of the distant past.\nMike Williams: Today we are surrounded by images. Does graffiti still have the impact that it did?\nProf Richard Clay: I think it can, I really do think it can. I think the power of graffiti is that when it’s put in a public space and it’s a clearly political statement, it’s a challenge to everybody who walks by, a challenge to their assumptions. I think perhaps we see in some of the arrests that go on all over the world, of graffiti artists, the fact that the authorities are anxious about some of the scrawlings on the walls, it’s very clear that artists are being targeted because their political views are unacceptable.\nThe illicitness is an interesting point. Looking at Roman history and we look at Pompeii and Pompeii is amazing because courtesy of Vesuvius, so much graffiti survives. What I was surprised to find out was that in the townhouses in Pompeii, about 80% of the house was actually open to the public and they would leave graffiti on the walls and it would be tolerated. And in fact, to clean the graffiti off the walls of your house in the public space, as we’d understand it, would be seen as a sign of weakness.\nMike Williams: It’s interesting that many of the graffiti writers I’ve spoken to, what they do is write their own name. That’s the point of it, to leave their tag. What’s that about? Why?\nProf Richard Clay: There are certain people who find society quite frustrating and quite alienating and, in any society, whether it’s the church or the government or the authorities, there are certain rules about who can leave marks where and for some individuals, that’s frustrating. And I think that graffiti artists, their urge to leave a mark is pushing back a little bit at the dominant voice."
